{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "c2",
      "status": "contradicted",
      "witness": {
        "rule": "R_RANKING",
        "operation": "RANK",
        "quote": "The strongest driver is the repayment history: the largest recorded repayment-status code across April-September 2005 was 1.0, indicating a payment delay of 1 month, which raises the risk score relative to the model's reference.",
        "evidence_ids": ["a1", "a2", "a3", "a4"],
        "claimed_result": "repayment history is the strongest driver",
        "alleged_conflict": "The attribution for 'pay_max (derived)' is 0.3622, but 'BILL_AMT1' has a larger absolute contribution of 0.2898? No — wait, 0.3622 > 0.2898, so repayment history is numerically strongest. But the claim says 'the largest recorded repayment-status code... is the strongest driver' — this conflates a fact (f1) with its derived feature (pay_max). The evidence attributes to the feature group 'pay_max (derived)', not to the fact f1. The claim misattributes the driver to the underlying fact, not the model's feature. However, the magnitude is correct. But the claim also says 'the largest recorded repayment-status code... is the strongest driver' — this is R_INVENTED because the model does not attribute to 'largest recorded repayment-status code' as a factor; it attributes to 'pay_max (derived)', which is a derived feature. The claim invents a factor name not in the evidence."
      },
      "reason": "The claim violates R_INVENTED by referring to 'the largest recorded repayment-status code' as the driver — this is a fact (f1), not a model feature. The model attributes to 'pay_max (derived)', which is a derived feature. The claim also violates R_FACT_AS_DRIVER by treating the observed fact (f1) as if it has an attribution, when only the derived feature (a1) does. The claim also violates R_RANKING if interpreted as saying repayment history is the strongest — but 0.3622 (pay_max) is indeed the largest absolute contribution, so R_RANKING is not violated. However, the phrasing 'the largest recorded repayment-status code' is not a model feature; it is an observed fact. The model does not attribute to facts, only to feature groups. Therefore, the claim invents a driver (R_INVENTED) and misattributes causality to a fact (R_FACT_AS_DRIVER)."
    }
  ],
  "omissions": [
    {
      "evidence_ids": ["p1"],
      "reason": "The explanation does not explicitly state the horizon definition ('the payment due after the September 2005 statement (next month); not 12 months') as documented in p1, though it correctly states the timing. This is not an omission under R_OMISSION because the probability and strongest driver are conveyed. R_OMISSION only applies if the probability estimate or strongest attribution is missing — both are present."
    },
    {
      "evidence_ids": ["a1"],
      "reason": "The strongest attribution (0.3622 from pay_max) is correctly identified as the driver, but its magnitude in raw log-odds is not stated in the explanation. R_OMISSION requires omission of the probability estimate or the strongest attribution — the strongest attribution is mentioned (repayment history raises risk) but its numerical value (0.3622) is omitted. This is an omission under R_OMISSION."
    }
  ]
}

